L000383	In this Supplementary Material, we provide a detailed analysis of the semi-classical limit for multi-photon light-matter interactions, and we derive the connection between heat currents and power in the different models employed in the main text. Throughout the Supplementary Material, we work in the rotating frame relative to the coherent drive unless explicitly stated.
L000384	
L000385	\section{Semi-classical expansion}
L000386	We present a systematic semi-classical expansion for a general quantum system coupled to a driven--dissipative cavity via a multi-photon interaction.
L000387	Our goal is to obtain the long-time dynamics in the limit of a large coherent cavity field and weak cavity--system coupling, and to show how the semi-classical model emerges.
L000388	We first illustrate the expansion for a flip--flop interaction and then extend the argument to the multi-photon interaction.
L000389	Finally, we detail how the average power in the IO framework recovers the semi-classical value.
L000390	\subsection{General setup}
L000391	
L000392	In a frame rotating at the cavity drive frequency, the Hamiltonian takes the form
L000393	\begin{align}
L000394	H &= H' + H_0 + V, \\
L000395	H_0 &= \Delta\, a^\dagger a
L000396	+ i\mathcal{E}\big(a^\dagger - a\big), \\
L000397	V &=
L000398	g \sum_{n=0}^N \sum_{m=0}^M
L000399	c_{nm}\, a^{n}(a^\dagger)^m \otimes O_{nm}
L000400	+ \text{H.c.},
L000401	\label{eq:general-interaction}
L000402	\end{align}
L000403	where $H'$ acts on the system Hilbert space and the $O_{nm}$ are system operators.
L000404	We assume $c_{nm}\in\mathbb{R}$ and of order unity; the integers $N,M$ denote the highest powers of $a$ and $a^\dagger$ appearing in the interaction.
L000405	The special case $N=1$, $M=0$ is the flip--flop interaction.
L000406	
L000407	The dynamics is governed by the Lindblad master equation
L000408	\begin{align}
L000409	\dv{\rho}{t} = -i[H,\rho]
L000410	+ \mathcal{D}\rho + \mathcal{D}'\rho,
L000411	\end{align}
L000412	where $\mathcal{D}$ describes cavity damping and $\mathcal{D}'$ collects all dissipative channels acting directly on the system.
L000413	For the cavity we take
L000414	\begin{align}
L000415	\mathcal{D}\rho
L000416	= \kappa(\nb+1)D[a]\rho
L000417	+ \kappa\nb\,D[a^\dagger]\rho,
L000418	\end{align}
L000419	with $\nb$ the Bose--Einstein occupation of the cavity bath, and $\mathcal{D}'$ is left general.
L000420	
L000421	We study the semi-classical regime
L000422	\begin{align}
L000423	\big|\alpha\big|\to\infty, \qquad \frac{g}{\kappa}\to0, \qquad \frac{g}{\kappa} \big|\alpha\big|^{N+M} =\text{const.}
L000424	\label{eq:semi-classical-limit}
L000425	\end{align}
L000426	We treat $g/\kappa$ as order $\epsilon$, while $|\alpha|$ is order $\epsilon^{-1/(N+M)}$.
L000427	This scaling ensures that the leading coherent contribution to the interaction,
L000428	$g\,\alpha^N(\alpha^*)^M O_{NM}$, remains finite.
L000429	
L000430	\subsection{Flip--flop interaction}
L000431	We explain the expansion for
L000432	\begin{align}
L000433	V = g(aO^\dagger + a^\dagger O).
L000434	\end{align}
L000435	The semi-classical model is obtained in three steps.
L000436	
L000437	
L000438	\textbf{1. Displacement.} Introduce the shifted operator
L000439	\begin{align}
L000440	\tilde{a} = a - \alpha,
L000441	\end{align}
L000442	where $\alpha \in \mathbb{C}$ is chosen later.
L000443	After substitution, the master equation becomes
L000444	\begin{align}
L000445	\dv{\rho}{t} = -i[\tilde{H},\rho] + \tilde{\mathcal{D}}\rho + \mathcal{D}'\rho,
L000446	\end{align}
L000447	where
L000448	\begin{align}
L000449	\tilde{H} &= \tilde{H}' + \tilde{H}_0 + \tilde{V},\\[2pt]
L000450	\tilde{H}_0
L000451	&= \Delta\, \tilde{a}^\dagger \tilde{a}
L000452	+ i\mathcal{E}(\tilde{a}-\tilde{a}^\dagger)
L000453	+ \Delta(\alpha^*\tilde{a} + \alpha\,\tilde{a}^\dagger)
L000454	+ \frac{\kappa}{2i}(\alpha^*\tilde{a} - \alpha \tilde{a}^\dagger),
L000455	\label{eq:cavity-shifted}\\
L000456	H_\text{sc} &= H' + g(\alpha O^\dagger + \alpha^* O),\\
L000457	\tilde{V} &= g(\tilde{a}O^\dagger + \tilde{a}^\dagger O),\label{eq:Vtilde}
L000458	\end{align}
L000459	and
L000460	\begin{align}
L000461	\tilde{\mathcal{D}}\rho
L000462	= \kappa(\nb+1)D[\tilde{a}]\rho
L000463	+ \kappa\nb\,D[\tilde{a}^\dagger]\rho,
L000464	\end{align}
L000465	using the invariance of linear Lindblad operators under affine shifts~\cite{landi_2024, schrauwen2025}.
L000466	
L000467	\textbf{2. Fixing the displacement.} We choose $\alpha$ such that all terms linear in $\tilde{a}$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:cavity-shifted} vanish.
L000468	This ensures that the displaced cavity mode relaxes to a zero-mean steady state,
L000469	\[
L000470	\langle \tilde{a} \rangle = 0 + \mathcal{O}(\epsilon),
L000471	\]
L000472	so that $\tilde{a}$ can be treated as a fluctuation operator.
L000473	Solving the cancellation condition gives
L000474	\begin{align}
L000475	\alpha = -2\,\frac{\mathcal{E}}{\kappa}\, \chi, \qquad
L000476	\chi = \frac{1}{1+2i\Delta/\kappa}.
L000477	\end{align}
L000478	Thus,
L000479	\begin{align}
L000480	\tilde{H}_0 &= \Delta\,\tilde{a}^\dagger\tilde{a},\\
L000481	H_\text{sc} &= H' + i(\mathcal{E}_\text{sc} O^\dagger - \mathcal{E}_\text{sc}^* O),\\
L000482	\mathcal{E}_\text{sc} &= -2 g\,\frac{\mathcal{E}}{\kappa}\, \chi.
L000483	\end{align}
L000484	The system therefore experiences an effective coherent drive of amplitude $\mathcal{E}_\text{sc}$.
L000485	
L000486	\textbf{3. Power counting and tracing out the cavity.} Inspecting Eq.~\eqref{eq:Vtilde}, we have that
L000487	\begin{align}
L000488	\tilde{V}/\kappa = \mathcal{O}(\epsilon),
L000489	\end{align}
L000490	since from Eq.~\eqref{eq:semi-classical-limit} $g/\kappa$ is order $\epsilon$, where we further  assume that the parameters of the system are kept fixed. The dissipative term $\tilde{\mathcal{D}}/\kappa$ is irrelevant upon tracing out the cavity.
L000491	Therefore, to first order in $\epsilon$, the reduced system dynamics is governed by
L000492	\begin{align}
L000493	\dv{\rho'}{t}
L000494	= -i[H_\text{sc},\rho'] + \mathcal{D}'\rho',
L000495	\end{align}
L000496	which matches the semi-classical master equation used in the main text. Formally, we have that in the semi-classical limit,
L000497	\begin{align}
L000498	    \expval{\dv{\rho'}{t} A} = \expval{\dv{\rho}{t} A} + \mathcal{O}(\epsilon),
L000499	\end{align}
L000500	for any system operator $A$.
L000501	
L000502	\subsection{Multi-photon interaction}
L000503	
L000504	We now extend the expansion to the full interaction~\eqref{eq:general-interaction}.
L000505	The displacement $\alpha$ remains unchanged because the cancellation of linear $\tilde{a}$ terms depends only on the cavity drive and damping.
L000506	
L000507	Substituting $a=\alpha+\tilde{a}$ into $V$ and using the binomial expansion yields
L000508	\begin{align}
L000509	\frac{V}{\kappa}
L000510	&= \frac{g}{\kappa}
L000511	\sum_{n,m}\sum_{k,l}
L000512	\binom{n}{k}\binom{m}{l}
L000513	c_{nm}\,
L000514	\alpha^{k}\tilde{a}^{\,n-k}
L000515	(\alpha^*)^{l}(\tilde{a}^\dagger)^{m-l}
L000516	\otimes O_{nm}
L000517	+ \text{H.c.} \\
L000518	&= \frac{H_\text{sc}}{\kappa}
L000519	+ \frac{\tilde{V}}{\kappa}
L000520	+ \mathcal{O}(\epsilon^2),
L000521	\end{align}
L000522	where we have used the scaling in Eq.~\eqref{eq:semi-classical-limit}.
L000523	The leading contribution is
L000524	\begin{align}
L000525	H_\text{sc}
L000526	&= g
L000527	c_{NM}\,\alpha^{N}(\alpha^*)^{M} O_{NM}
L000528	+ \text{H.c.},
L000529	\end{align}
L000530	which is finite by construction of the semi-classical limit,
L000531	\begin{align}
L000532	\frac{H_\text{sc}}{\kappa} \propto \frac{g}{\kappa} |\alpha|^{N+M}.
L000533	\end{align}
L000534	Terms containing a single power of $\tilde{a}$ or $\tilde{a}^\dagger$ contribute to
L000535	\begin{align}
L000536	\tilde{V}
L000537	&= g
L000538	\Big[
L000539	N\,c_{NM}\,\alpha^{N-1}(\alpha^*)^{M} \tilde{a}\, O_{NM}
L000540	+ M\,c_{NM}\,\alpha^{N}(\alpha^*)^{M-1} \tilde{a}^\dagger\, O_{NM}
L000541	\Big]
L000542	+ \text{H.c.},
L000543	\end{align}
L000544	and scale as $\mathcal{O}(\epsilon)$, akin to the flip-flop interaction discussed above.
L000545	
L000546	This shows that, up to prefactors and system operators, the structure of $\tilde{V}$ reduces to a flip--flop coupling in the semi-classical limit, and matches the simpler form discussed previously when $N=1$, $M=0$.
L000547	Higher-order contributions, involving two or more powers of $\tilde{a}$ or $\tilde{a}^\dagger$, are of order $\epsilon^2$ or smaller and are irrelevant in the semi-classical limit.
L000548	
L000549	Hence the semi-classical expansion and resulting effective dynamics derived for the flip--flop model extend directly to general cavity--system interactions, with only the explicit form of the induced drive $H_\text{sc}$ and residual coupling $\tilde{V}$ modified.
L000550	Therefore, without loss of generality, we can focus on the flip--flop interaction in the main text, and in the developments below.
L000551	
L000552	\section{Average power in the semi-classical limit}
L000553	
L000554	The standard definition of power given in the lab frame $\langle P \rangle  = \langle \partial_t H(t)\rangle$, implies that, in the rotating frame,
L000555	\begin{align}
L000556	    \langle P \rangle = -\omega_\text{d} \mathcal{E}\, \langle a + a^\dagger \rangle.
L000557	\end{align}
L000558	In contrast, the IO definitions presented in the main text via in/out fields can be cast into the language of a master equation via a transformation $a \to a - \langle a \rangle$ in the cavity dissipators,
L000559	\begin{align}
L000560	    \mathcal{D}[a] &\to \mathcal{D}_s[a] = \mathcal{D}[a - \langle a \rangle]\\
L000561	    H(t) &\to H_s(t) = H(t) + i\frac{\kappa}{2}\big(\langle a^\dagger\rangle a - \langle a\rangle a^\dagger\big),
L000562	\end{align}
L000563	This transformation leaves the Lindblad equation invariant, but modifies the identification of coherent and incoherent processes.
L000564	
L000565	The IO power is then defined in the lab frame as,
L000566	\begin{align}
L000567	    \langle P_\text{io}\rangle = \langle\partial_t H_s(t)\rangle.
L000568	\end{align}
L000569	which is  equivalent to Eq.~(\ref{eq:Pio})~\cite{schrauwen2025}.
L000570	In the lab frame, we can set $\partial_t \langle a\rangle \simeq -i\omega_\text{d} \langle a \rangle$~\cite{schrauwen2025}, and we find
L000571	\begin{align}
L000572	\langle P_\text{io} \rangle  &= \langle P\rangle  -\omega_\text{d}\kappa\big|\langle a \rangle\big|^2.
L000573	\end{align}
L000574	
L000575	In the long-time limit, we obtain from the Heisenberg equations of motion for $a$,
L000576	\begin{align}
L000577	    \langle a \rangle &= \alpha -2 i \frac{g}{\kappa} \chi\, \langle O\rangle ,\\
L000578	    \alpha &= -2\frac{\mathcal{E}}{\kappa}\chi.
L000579	\end{align}
L000580	Substituting into $\langle P \rangle$ yields
L000581	\begin{align}
L000582	    \langle P \rangle
L000583	    &= 4\omega_\text{d} \frac{\mathcal{E}^2|\chi|^2}{\kappa}
L000584	    + 2i\omega_\text{d} \mathcal{E}\frac{g}{\kappa}\langle O \chi - O^\dagger \chi^* \rangle,
L000585	\end{align}
L000586	where we used that
L000587	\begin{align}
L000588	    \Re \chi = |\chi|^2
L000589	    \quad\Rightarrow\quad
L000590	    \chi^* = 2|\chi|^2 - \chi,\qquad
L000591	    \chi = 2|\chi|^2 - \chi^*.
L000592	\end{align}
L000593	Using these relations, we can rewrite
L000594	\begin{align}
L000595	\langle P \rangle &=  \langle P_\text{sc} \rangle + 4\omega_\text{d} \frac{\mathcal{E}^2|\chi|^2}{\kappa}  + 8 \omega_\text{d} \mathcal{E}|\chi|^2\frac{g}{\kappa}\Im \langle O\rangle,
L000596	\label{eq:sm-P-scaling}
L000597	\end{align}
L000598	where, for the semi-classical model in the rotating frame,
L000599	\begin{align}
L000600	    \tilde{H}_\text{d}&= g ( \alpha O^\dagger + \alpha^* O),\\
L000601	    \langle P_\text{sc} \rangle  &= -i \omega_\text{d} g  \,\langle\alpha O^\dagger - \alpha^*O\rangle\\
L000602	            &=-2i\omega_\text{d}\mathcal{E}\frac{g}{\kappa} \langle \chi^*O - \chi O^\dagger \rangle.\nonumber
L000603	\end{align}
L000604	
L000605	For the IO power, we have
L000606	\begin{align}
L000607	    \langle P_\text{io} \rangle - \langle P \rangle
L000608	    &=  -\omega_\text{d}\kappa\big|\langle a \rangle\big|^2 \\
L000609	    &= -\omega_\text{d}\kappa |\alpha|^2
L000610	    + 2i \omega_\text{d} g \big[\alpha^* \chi\,\langle O\rangle - \alpha \chi^* \langle O^\dagger\rangle\big]
L000611	    + \mathcal{O}\!\left(\epsilon^2\right) \nonumber\\
L000612	     &= -\omega_\text{d}\kappa |\alpha|^2
L000613	     - 8 \omega_\text{d} \mathcal{E}|\chi|^2\frac{g}{\kappa}\Im\langle O \rangle
L000614	    + \mathcal{O}\!\left(\epsilon^2\right). \nonumber
L000615	\end{align}
L000616	We show below that the right-hand side of the last equation above is simply $\langle J \rangle + \mathcal{O}(\epsilon^2)$, which leads to Eqs.~(\ref{eq:P_to_sc},~\ref{eq:J_to_sc}) in the main text.
L000617	Combining the equation above with Eq.~\eqref{eq:sm-P-scaling}, and using that $\mathcal{E}/\kappa \propto |\alpha|$ and $\langle O \rangle = \mathcal{O}(1)$
L000618	, we obtain
L000619	\begin{align}
L000620	\langle P_\text{io}\rangle = \langle P_\text{sc}\rangle + \mathcal{O}(\epsilon^2).
L000621	\label{eq:sm-Pio-scaling}
L000622	\end{align}
L000623	Thus, the IO definition of power recovers the semi-classical value up to corrections of order $\epsilon^2$, whereas the standard definition differs by an extensive contribution stemming from the coherent cavity field.
L000624	
L000625	\section{Heat currents and entropy production}
L000626	
L000627	Heat currents in a general open quantum system can be defined by introducing a thermodynamic Hamiltonian~\cite{Potts_2021, potts2024quantumthermodynamics, schrauwen2025},
L000628	\begin{align}
L000629	    H_\text{TD} = \omega_\text{d} a^\dagger a + H_\text{TD}',
L000630	\end{align}
L000631	where $H_\text{TD}'$ depends on the details of the embedded system.
L000632	Writing the system dissipator as a sum of thermal channels,
L000633	\begin{align}
L000634	    \mathcal{D}' = \sum_j \mathcal{D}_j,
L000635	\end{align}
L000636	with $\mathcal{D}_j$ encompassing thermal dissipators, each related to a bath at temperature $T_j$, the average heat current associated with the $j$-th channel is
L000637	\begin{align}
L000638	    \langle J_j \rangle = \tr\big(H_\text{TD} \mathcal{D}_j \rho\big) = \tr\big(H_\text{TD}' \mathcal{D}_j \rho\big),
L000639	\end{align}
L000640	where additive constants in $H_\text{TD}$ have no effect.
L000641	For the cavity,
L000642	\begin{align}
L000643	    \langle J \rangle = \tr\big(H_\text{TD}\mathcal{D}\rho\big) = \omega_\text{d}\,\tr\big(a^\dagger a\,\mathcal{D}\rho\big)=\omega_\text{d} \kappa (\nb - \langle a^\dagger a\rangle).
L000644	\end{align}
L000645	In the three-level maser case discussed in the main text,
L000646	\begin{align}
L000647	    H_\text{TD}' = \omega_3 \dyad{3}{3} + (\omega_3  - \omega_\text{d}) \dyad{2}{2},
L000648	\end{align}
L000649	and the thermal dissipators are $\mathcal{D}_\text{H}, \mathcal{D}_\text{C}$ introduced in Eqs.~(\ref{eq:DH},~\ref{eq:DC}).
L000650	In this section we keep $H_\text{TD}'$ and $\mathcal{D}_j$ general.
L000651	
L000652	Combining the results of the previous section, we now argue that these general definitions of heat currents lead to the general results quoted in the main text for thermodynamic uncertainties.
L000653	In the IO framework, $H_\text{TD}$ is unchanged, while the cavity dissipator transforms as $\mathcal{D}\to\mathcal{D}_s$.
L000654	Therefore, the heat currents in the intra-cavity system are unchanged.
L000655	
L000656	Using Eq.~\eqref{eq:sm-Pio-scaling} and the assumptions entering its derivation, we obtain
L000657	\begin{align}
L000658	    \langle J_\text{io} \rangle = \mathcal{O}(\epsilon^2).
L000659	\end{align}
L000660	
L000661	
L000662	Moreover, the following equality holds~\cite{schrauwen2025},
L000663	\begin{align}
L000664	    \langle J \rangle + \langle P \rangle = \langle J_\text{io} \rangle + \langle P_\text{io} \rangle,
L000665	\end{align}
L000666	which expresses the invariance of the total energy balance under the shift introduced in the IO framework.
L000667	This implies that the mismatch in power between the frameworks is accompanied by an equal and opposite mismatch in the cavity heat current.
L000668	Using the semi-classical limit and long-time limit, we find
L000669	\begin{align}
L000670	   \langle J \rangle = -\kappa \omega_\text{d}|\alpha|^2 - 8 \omega_\text{d} \mathcal{E}|\chi|^2\frac{g}{\kappa}\Im \langle O \rangle + \mathcal{O}(\epsilon^2).\label{eq:heat-expansion}
L000671	\end{align}
L000672	
L000673	
L000674	
L000675	Thus, in the semi-classical limit, the standard definition of the cavity heat current retains an extensive contribution incompatible with its semi-classical counterpart, whereas the IO definition remains compatible.
L000676	
L000677	These differences translate directly to entropy production rates and thermodynamic uncertainties for system currents.
L000678	Denoting by $\sigma$, $\sigma_\text{io}$ and $\sigma_\text{sc}$ the entropy production rates obtained from the standard, IO, and semi-classical frameworks respectively, we find for any system current $J_j$,
L000679	\begin{align}
L000680	    \mathcal{Q}(J_j, \sigma_\text{io}) &= \mathcal{Q}(J_j, \sigma_\text{sc}) + \mathcal{O}(\epsilon^2), \\
L000681	    \mathcal{Q}(J_j, \sigma) &= \mathcal{Q}(J_j, \sigma_\text{sc}) -  \frac{\langle \!\langle J_j^2 \rangle\!\rangle}{\langle J_j \rangle ^2} \frac{\langle J \rangle }{T}
L000682	    + \mathcal{O}(\epsilon^2).
L000683	\end{align}
L000684	
L000685	In deriving these relations we used the semi-classical and long-time limits, as well as the flip--flop interaction structure.
L000686	They generalise to the multi-photon interaction~\eqref{eq:general-interaction} by approximating the master equation in the semi-classical regime~\eqref{eq:semi-classical-limit} and consistently applying the power counting developed above.
